utside-spending surge on his behalf, would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 59 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent John McGuire (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 5th District leans Republican by about six points, and first-term incumbent John McGuire is seeking re-election with a solid fundraising base. Democratic challenger Tom Perriello, a former congressman from the district, has actually out-raised McGuire and entered the fall with more cash on hand, and he has drawn endorsements from a former Republican congressman and a 'Republicans for Tom' group, though an AOC endorsement got a lukewarm local reception.\n\nExpert raters mostly call the race Likely or Lean Republican, and prediction markets give Perriello roughly a one-in-five chance. The only public poll, a Democratic-sponsored August survey, had McGuire up three. The forecast lands at a Republican margin of about five points, with wide uncertainty given the thin and dated polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1914, "margin": -5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2771, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2238, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2476, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2003, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1957, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2343, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2412, "margin": -5.0}]}